{"type":"document","version":"0.5","doc":"readme","source":"README.md","sha256":"93c9e8ea05570f23b3780f2c4714abfc13a7f9bfe979cd8bee1c734882367f35","protection":"none","signed":false,"converter":"fmjl 0.5","structure":true,"elements":42,"created":"2026-09-29T10:26:16Z","lang":"en","access":["all"],"last_id":61}
{"id":"readme#e1","hash":"5636dbcbeadd314d","type":"heading","level":1,"parent":null,"characters":6,"md":"# FMJL"}
{"id":"readme#e26","hash":"13fb731430b0a6e6","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e1","characters":422,"md":"FMJL turns any document into a list of small parts that a search engine or a RAG system can\nuse directly: every heading, paragraph, table, formula, image and caption becomes one line\nwith a stable id, a content hash, its page and position on the page, and who may read it.\nPeople never see those lines: they write and edit the same document as normal Markdown, and\nthe tool converts both ways without renumbering anything."}
{"id":"readme#e2","hash":"aef3f50b2cc4ddfc","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e1","characters":134,"md":"FMJL, short for Format, Markdown, JSON Lines, is a document format that combines four\nlanguages, each doing the one job it is best at:"}
{"id":"readme#e3","hash":"6080d801b6fff20a","type":"table","parent":"readme#e1","characters":670,"md":"| Language | Job in FMJL | Why that one |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| JSON Lines | The container: one element per line, with its id, hash, page, position and permissions | Every programming language reads JSON; one line per element means a file can be streamed, searched and cut without a parser |\n| Markdown | All readable text: headings, paragraphs, lists, simple tables, image descriptions | People can read and write it directly; it is the language of GitHub, VS Code and every editor |\n| LaTeX | Math formulas | The only language that writes every formula exactly |\n| HTML | Tables with merged cells or multi-level headers | Markdown tables cannot merge cells; HTML can |"}
{"id":"readme#e27","hash":"1fefa7de46920d70","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":22,"md":"## Install in one line"}
{"id":"readme#e28","hash":"489f5aa0447599ce","type":"code","parent":"readme#e27","characters":160,"md":"```powershell\npip install fmjl            # converter, checker, Word importer, retriever chunks\npip install \"fmjl[pdf]\"     # the same plus the PDF importer\n```"}
{"id":"readme#e29","hash":"66e337df919547f9","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e27","characters":184,"md":"No Python? Download `fmjl.exe` from the Releases page and put it on your PATH. In VS Code,\ninstall the **FMJL** extension (publisher rupakkumar): it converts and checks without Python."}
{"id":"readme#e30","hash":"f3e788fe11faa0c9","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":22,"md":"## Convert in one line"}
{"id":"readme#e31","hash":"b6258cf520da01b8","type":"code","parent":"readme#e30","characters":382,"md":"```powershell\nfmjl notes.md               # Markdown  -> notes.fmjl, then checks every rule\nfmjl notes.fmjl             # .fmjl     -> notes.md; edit it and run the first line again\nfmjl report.pdf             # PDF       -> report.fmjl, report.md and images/\nfmjl report.docx            # Word      -> the same\nfmjl notes.md out\\notes.fmjl    # a second name is the output file\n```"}
{"id":"readme#e32","hash":"6c6dab1e93481890","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e30","characters":152,"md":"The file extension tells the tool which way to go. Elements you did not change keep their\nids and hashes, so a vector store only re-embeds what changed."}
{"id":"readme#e33","hash":"a2292cc518ead757","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":26,"md":"## Use in RAG in ten lines"}
{"id":"readme#e34","hash":"6f6c871fb821bd1c","type":"code","parent":"readme#e33","characters":600,"md":"```python\nimport fmjl\n\nrows = fmjl.load(\"report.fmjl\")                     # header first, then one dict per element\nfor chunk in fmjl.chunks(rows, by=\"section\"):       # or by=\"element\", the default\n    store.add(embed(chunk[\"text\"]), metadata={\n        \"id\": chunk[\"id\"], \"page\": chunk.get(\"page\"), \"bbox\": chunk.get(\"bbox\"),\n        \"access\": chunk[\"access\"], \"hash\": chunk[\"hash\"]})\n\nnew = fmjl.chunks(fmjl.load(\"report_v2.fmjl\"), by=\"section\")\nfor chunk in fmjl.changed_chunks(new, fmjl.chunks(rows, by=\"section\")):\n    store.replace(chunk[\"id\"], embed(chunk[\"text\"]))    # only what changed\n```"}
{"id":"readme#e35","hash":"b5c987b05508f89d","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e33","characters":604,"md":"Each chunk's `text` is the heading path above it, a blank line, then the content, with\ncaptions and footnotes attached to what they describe. Filter by `access` before searching,\nand cite answers with the element id and `page` plus 1. The same is available from the\ncommand line as `fmjl chunks report.fmjl [--by section] [--since old.fmjl]`.\n`examples/rag_demo.py` is the whole pipeline in one file with nothing to install: it\nscores the chunks of every `.fmjl` in a folder against a question, prints the best ones\nwith their citation, and asks Claude when the `anthropic` package and a key are present."}
{"id":"readme#e36","hash":"82c2f8ec529e9997","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":25,"md":"## What a file looks like"}
{"id":"readme#e37","hash":"3bf126b8e44e2a6e","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e36","characters":44,"md":"Two lines of the storage form, `notes.fmjl`:"}
{"id":"readme#e38","hash":"c23c30b339d2df5d","type":"code","parent":"readme#e36","characters":475,"md":"```json\n{\"type\":\"document\",\"version\":\"0.5\",\"doc\":\"notes\",\"source\":\"notes.md\",\"sha256\":\"...\",\"protection\":\"none\",\"signed\":false,\"converter\":\"fmjl 0.5\",\"structure\":true,\"elements\":2,\"created\":\"2026-09-29T10:00:00Z\",\"lang\":\"en\",\"access\":[\"all\"],\"last_id\":2}\n{\"id\":\"notes#e2\",\"hash\":\"b91ad9f6b39e617a\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"parent\":\"notes#e1\",\"page\":0,\"bbox\":[80,110,920,170],\"characters\":105,\"md\":\"This policy applies to all full-time employees from their first day of work.\"}\n```"}
{"id":"readme#e39","hash":"f75c3cc63429b868","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e36","characters":170,"md":"The same document in the authoring form, `notes.md`, is plain Markdown with a small hidden\nnote before each block that carries the id and any fields Markdown cannot hold:"}
{"id":"readme#e40","hash":"50662cc447aaccd8","type":"code","parent":"readme#e36","characters":131,"md":"```markdown\n<!-- e2 page=0 bbox=80,110,920,170 -->\nThis policy applies to all full-time employees from their first day of work.\n```"}
{"id":"readme#e6","hash":"51f781aba764f1b8","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e36","characters":58,"md":"One document has two forms that hold the same information:"}
{"id":"readme#e7","hash":"4ce72eb5c51ef077","type":"table","parent":"readme#e36","characters":171,"md":"| Form | File | Made for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Storage | `name.fmjl` | Machines: search, RAG, databases |\n| Authoring | `name.md` | People: reading, writing, reviewing |"}
{"id":"readme#e41","hash":"243b39c9e874f73f","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e36","characters":275,"md":"Merged cells never need HTML by hand. In a Markdown table a cell holding only `^` joins\nthe cell above it and a cell holding only `<` joins the cell to its left; the tool writes\nthe HTML with `rowspan` and `colspan` for you, and shows the same shortcut when you\nconvert back:"}
{"id":"readme#e42","hash":"5188cde42237e93f","type":"code","parent":"readme#e36","characters":99,"md":"```markdown\n| Name | Score | < |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ^ | Math | AI |\n| Rupak Kumar | 9 | 10 |\n```"}
{"id":"readme#e43","hash":"9305655a318ba0dc","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e36","characters":192,"md":"A caption points at the block next to it with `<!-- type=caption reference=above -->`.\nThe rulebook in `rulebook/` lists all 20 element types, every field, and every rule the\nchecker enforces."}
{"id":"readme#e44","hash":"011a26bd69a74ed1","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":28,"md":"## From a PDF or a Word file"}
{"id":"readme#e45","hash":"66a9f4a7d4ddb8e5","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e44","characters":525,"md":"The PDF importer reads the text layer and turns it into elements: headings by font size,\nparagraphs, lists, tables with merged cells, charts and pictures with their captions\nlinked by `reference`, and repeated headers, footers and page numbers marked as `noise`.\nEvery element carries `page` (counted from 0) and `bbox` (0 to 1000), so an answer can\npoint at the exact place on the page. Open the `.md` afterwards, fix what the importer\ngot wrong, and run `fmjl report.md`; the ids stay. Scanned pages need Tesseract for OCR."}
{"id":"readme#e46","hash":"0dbbeb71608cb71e","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e44","characters":383,"md":"The Word importer reads the document's own structure: Heading styles, bullet and numbered\nlists, tables with merged cells, images with captions, footnotes, Word formulas as LaTeX,\nthe header and footer as `noise`, and page numbers from the page breaks Word recorded.\nTracked changes are accepted with a warning. `examples/` holds a sample of each with its\nimported `.fmjl` and `.md`."}
{"id":"readme#e47","hash":"13fdfbd78611c8c8","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":15,"md":"## All commands"}
{"id":"readme#e48","hash":"4e686cad8da62ea2","type":"table","parent":"readme#e47","characters":762,"md":"| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `fmjl new notes.md` | Authoring form to storage form |\n| `fmjl md notes.fmjl` | Storage form to authoring form |\n| `fmjl fill notes.fmjl` | Fills in `id`, `hash`, `characters` and `parent`; makes `md` canonical |\n| `fmjl check notes.fmjl` | Checks every rule of the rulebook |\n| `fmjl view notes.fmjl` | Prints the document as clean Markdown |\n| `fmjl info notes.fmjl` | Prints the title, element counts and an outline |\n| `fmjl upgrade old.fmjl` | Turns a version 0.1 to 0.4 file into 0.5 |\n| `fmjl pdf report.pdf` | PDF to storage form, authoring form and `images/` |\n| `fmjl docx report.docx` | Word to storage form, authoring form and `images/` |\n| `fmjl chunks report.fmjl` | Retriever-ready chunks as JSON Lines |"}
{"id":"readme#e49","hash":"0731b75db3889213","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e47","characters":143,"md":"Without the package installed, `python fmjl.py <command>` from this folder does the same;\n`fmjl.cmd` here is the one-word shortcut for Windows."}
{"id":"readme#e50","hash":"8a0c868b4488af82","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":27,"md":"## What the benchmark shows"}
{"id":"readme#e51","hash":"76e9c313cc2bbf6c","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e50","characters":179,"md":"The same 75-sentence report with two formulas, a table, an image and a caption was written\nin FMJL, Markdown, JSON and LaTeX, then read back and scored (`benchmark/results.json`):"}
{"id":"readme#e18","hash":"e38dc600824f542f","type":"table","parent":"readme#e50","characters":383,"md":"|  | FMJL | Markdown | JSON | LaTeX |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Page number of every element | 23/23 | 0/23 | 23/23 | 0/23 |\n| Caption linked to its image | yes | no | yes | yes |\n| Elements keeping their id after an edit | 23/23 | 0/23 | 21/23 | 5/23 |\n| Detects a silently changed letter | yes | no | no | no |\n| Read one document | 0.11 ms | 3.85 ms | 0.07 ms | 29.32 ms |"}
{"id":"readme#e52","hash":"af5836c2a96f7821","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e50","characters":66,"md":"The same run imports the two sample files (rulebook section 10.1):"}
{"id":"readme#e53","hash":"aebb5c137b250374","type":"table","parent":"readme#e50","characters":363,"md":"|  | PDF, 3 pages | Word, 2 pages |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Elements found | 25 | 20 |\n| Captions linked to their image or table | 2/2 | 3/3 |\n| Tables with merged cells kept as HTML | 0 of 1 (none merged) | 1 of 2 |\n| Elements with a page | 25/25 | 20/20 |\n| Elements with a position on the page | 25/25 | 0/20 (Word has none) |\n| Passes `fmjl check` | yes | yes |"}
{"id":"readme#e54","hash":"3057bc0b6faea60d","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":10,"md":"## VS Code"}
{"id":"readme#e55","hash":"4f86c2195f1440f0","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e54","characters":510,"md":"Search for **FMJL** in the Extensions view and install it (publisher rupakkumar). It needs\nno Python. Right-click a `.md` file for **FMJL: Convert Markdown to .fmjl**, or a `.fmjl`\nfile for **FMJL: Convert .fmjl to Markdown**. Any `.fmjl` file gets coloring, red\nunderlines for rule violations, and the command **FMJL: Check current file**. The\nextension's converter is a JavaScript port of `fmjl.py`; `npm test` in `fmjl-vscode/`\nproves the two give byte-identical output on every document in this repository."}
{"id":"readme#e56","hash":"fa8248451f55650d","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":16,"md":"## Folder layout"}
{"id":"readme#e57","hash":"fe0f7ef631bb5f35","type":"code","parent":"readme#e56","characters":655,"md":"```text\nfmjl.py          the reference tool (needs Python 3.9+ and: pip install jsonschema)\nfmjl_pdf.py      the PDF importer (needs: pip install pymupdf)\nfmjl_docx.py     the Word importer (needs nothing else)\npyproject.toml   the PyPI package: pip install fmjl\nrulebook/        the specification, version 0.5, in both forms\nexamples/        sample documents (Markdown, PDF, Word) with their images/, and rag_demo.py\nfmjl-vscode/     the VS Code extension: convert, syntax coloring and live checking\nbenchmark/       the same document in FMJL, Markdown, JSON and LaTeX, and the scores\narchive/         older versions of the rulebook and the extension\n```"}
{"id":"readme#e58","hash":"3a8c9ed140cb0af3","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":9,"md":"## Status"}
{"id":"readme#e59","hash":"2a7d6312f112fcad","type":"paragraph","parent":"readme#e58","characters":247,"md":"Draft, version 0.5. The rulebook is the authority; if `fmjl.py` and the rulebook\ndisagree, the tool has a bug. Version 1.0 follows once the package, the extension and\nthe executable have been used on real documents by people other than the author."}
{"id":"readme#e60","hash":"dbe8a79f5ca44487","type":"heading","level":2,"parent":"readme#e1","characters":21,"md":"## Author and license"}
